#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07607d87-94c7-5615-8410-d21177e24ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506689} {:answer/id #uuid "6957dcc3-1d7f-5d45-8049-c8f637d3dde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506924} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff40b82-1458-59f6-ab1d-32e43630183c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507021} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb3a7af-4a78-5819-9be4-1fc493c69627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07607d87-94c7-5615-8410-d21177e24ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506689} :answer-field/id #uuid "09882a98-7e0e-5272-9868-612a26c52a00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af83bf7a-2326-5550-86bc-d86a83bd79bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343874" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-4({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{b}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)=\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7f8754-41a4-514c-86d1-d5647b54333b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{98\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506703} {:answer/id #uuid "a564bbb9-af52-596a-bcf1-bb101438423e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507039} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e53c4f-ca05-5e48-9e4b-fba6af34790c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507136} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ebfba2-6c04-5678-a8f3-079e02b2c158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{n(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3e53c4f-ca05-5e48-9e4b-fba6af34790c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507136} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a63a77-7e75-5948-b4a4-f7660df42d29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "776f8284-bdba-5ab6-8f5c-16f7e1be5e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-343877" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+49y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+49({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b056ad-c695-5840-bd81-265a5a05fdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506720} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae162dd-9009-571c-8a32-a223b4b316b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506779} {:answer/id #uuid "40e382b8-4b9d-57ca-b2bd-cc8b191c72b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506828} {:answer/id #uuid "7e847ab9-d2f2-5bd1-a314-8b4ab67840a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e847ab9-d2f2-5bd1-a314-8b4ab67840a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506956} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab909fc9-81ec-5132-a654-5b476b6505de" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110da6e6-f9ed-5990-8e21-185d0c9edf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506712} {:answer/id #uuid "22cce65c-606a-579a-a1d5-eed3f3596381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506751} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdbd401-7edc-5f4d-9c1c-340d4823c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507122} {:answer/id #uuid "edc46441-d5cb-51fb-9094-79f71f9202c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "110da6e6-f9ed-5990-8e21-185d0c9edf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506712} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9a6688-96f8-5a1f-9a15-b61299b1c973" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "735d1714-8711-57ac-8156-7342afc0beb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343900" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{9{\\pi }^{2}}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=3\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi A=0 \\\\ 6\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{54\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122300c0-651a-5232-86c6-033d51da03b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368364} {:answer/id #uuid "dd80f01e-6d8e-536a-823c-7056a3b8e1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368365} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2cdeac-9e0a-5c78-8be5-826656897a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368366} {:answer/id #uuid "94f19c63-b8e4-5cc8-bbcf-7867a07f6258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d2cdeac-9e0a-5c78-8be5-826656897a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368366} :answer-field/id #uuid "45d93abd-3293-5cd5-b7bf-40e46fb373a3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c19f30-8cb0-5c94-bc48-e088f92bd54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368369} {:answer/id #uuid "505b3f74-4f8e-58d3-bd80-0663f572435a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368370} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4b72a7-1c1f-532e-95af-dd53910e34d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368371} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff458e0-f735-5d03-a067-564058720262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "505b3f74-4f8e-58d3-bd80-0663f572435a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368370} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da0e00c-bb8a-5ae4-9075-989b21ab79e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5a48baf-9ceb-513a-885b-da45d3ee0d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-343911" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A=4 \\\\ \\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{4}{\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4163fb4e-1673-56b1-9e81-b3f9b76bdcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506831} {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc323db-b9be-5ba3-89bf-74f4864af6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507097} {:answer/id #uuid "ef817cb8-b7c8-5338-9ccb-b25eb7ba3fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} :answer-field/id #uuid "955e89a0-7f01-5af1-93d8-b8760f8a0f25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} {:answer/id #uuid "7380ffe2-b480-549e-b9ff-55f23f096330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506943} {:answer/id #uuid "c1abe79a-867b-5b61-b180-db62e3c89468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507105} {:answer/id #uuid "db644471-744d-5974-86ca-8c2d68c380d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28d2851-cb92-5497-aeb3-e6113495aa58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d683633f-d60f-57e1-b38c-1931a877a7fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-343914" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}]}
